<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-changing-the-subject" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Changing the Subject</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.0 Numbers and Algebra
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.5 Formulae and Variations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Rewrite a formula to change the subject in different situations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Suggested Time</title>
                    <p>
                        15 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Objective</title>
                    <p>
                        To allow learners to collaboratively discover the process of isolating a variable using a familiar linear formula and test their results empirically.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                    <p>
                        Circulate the room while groups discuss the formula <m>v = u + at</m>. Ensure learners are correctly identifying the operations binding the variables and prompt them to think about how they would undo those operations to isolate <m>t</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

    <activity>
    <title>Changing the Subject of a Formula</title>
    <statement>

        <p><strong>Class Project: The Math Club Fundraiser</strong></p>

        <p>
    Your class is running a Math Club fundraising project during Sports Day.
    To keep track of the money earned, you use the formula:
        </p>

        <me>P = S - C</me>

        <ul>
            <li><m>P</m> = Profit</li>
            <li><m>S</m> = Sales (money collected)</li>
            <li><m>C</m> = Costs (money spent)</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            In this formula, <m>P</m> is the subject because it stands alone on one side of the equation.
        </p>

        <p><strong>A New Challenge</strong></p>

        <p>
            Suppose your group wants to make a certain profit and already knows the costs.
            How can you find the sales needed?
        </p>

        <p>
            We need to make <m>S</m> the subject of the formula.
        </p>

        <p>Start with:</p>

        <me>P = S - C</me>

        <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Add <m>C</m> to both sides.</p>

        <me>P + C = S</me>

        <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Rewrite with <m>S</m> on the left.</p>

        <me>S = P + C</me>

        <p><strong>What does this mean?</strong></p>

        <p>
            To find the sales needed, add the profit you want to make to the costs you spent.
        </p>

        <me>S = P + C</me>

        <p><strong>Try It</strong></p>

        <p>
            Your group wants to make a profit of Ksh 500 and the costs are Ksh 300.
        </p>

        <p>Use the formula <m>S = P + C</m> to find the sales needed.</p>

        <p>
            Calculate the value of <m>S</m> and share your answer with your group.
        </p>

        <p><strong>Think About It</strong></p>

        <p>
            If the costs increase to Ksh 450 but the desired profit remains Ksh 500,
            how much money must the group collect in sales?
        </p>

    </statement>
</activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalize the concepts and properties learners discovered in the anchor task.
                </p>
            </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> Highlight how groups subtracted <m>u</m> first before dividing by <m>a</m> when isolating <m>t</m> from <m>v = u + at</m>. Explicitly link this to the rule of applying inverse operations in reverse BOMDAS/Order of Operations to systematic equation solving.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>
    <insight>
        <statement>
            <p><strong>Changing the Subject of a Formula</strong></p>
            <p>Changing the subject means rearranging a formula to make a chosen variable stand alone.</p>
            <p>Use inverse operations (add/subtract, multiply/divide, square/square root) to isolate the variable.</p>
            <p><em>Example:</em> <m>t = \frac{v - u}{a}</m></p>
            <p>Apply the same operation to both sides of the equation.</p>
            <p>This helps in solving equations and expressing formulas in different ways.</p>
        </statement>
    </insight>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
        
        <statement>
            <p>Given the formula <m>v^2 = u^2 + 2as</m>:</p>
            <ol>
                <li>Make <m>s</m> the subject of the formula.</li>
                <li>Find <m>s</m> when <m>v = 10</m>, <m>u = 2</m>, and <m>a = 6</m>.</li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <me>v^2 = u^2 + 2as</me>
                    <me>v^2 - u^2 = 2as</me>
                    <me>s = \frac{v^2 - u^2}{2a}</me>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <me>s = \frac{10^2 - 2^2}{2(6)}</me>
                    <me>s = \frac{100 - 4}{12}</me>
                    <me>s = \frac{96}{12}</me>
                    <me>s = 8</me>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
    
        <statement>
            <p>Given the formula <m>P = 2(l + w)</m>:</p>
            <ol>
                <li>Make <m>w</m> the subject of the formula.</li>
                <li>Find <m>w</m> when <m>P = 40</m> and <m>l = 12</m>.</li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <me>P = 2(l + w)</me>
                    <me>\frac{P}{2} = l + w</me>
                    <me>w = \frac{P}{2} - l</me>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <me>w = \frac{40}{2} - 12</me>
                    <me>w = 20 - 12</me>
                    <me>w = 8</me>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        
        <statement>
            <p>Given the formula <m>y = mx + c</m>:</p>
            <ol>
                <li>Make <m>x</m> the subject of the formula.</li>
                <li>Find <m>x</m> when <m>y = 20</m>, <m>m = 3</m>, and <m>c = 5</m>.</li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <me>y = mx + c</me>
                    <me>y - c = mx</me>
                    <me>x = \frac{y - c}{m}</me>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <me>x = \frac{20 - 5}{3}</me>
                    <me>x = \frac{15}{3}</me>
                    <me>x = 5</me>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Given the formula for the area of a trapezoid <m>A = \frac{1}{2}(a + b)h</m>:</p>
                <ol>
                    <li>Make <m>h</m> the subject of the formula.</li>
                    <li>Find <m>h</m> when <m>A = 30</m>, <m>a = 4</m>, and <m>b = 6</m>.</li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><strong>(a)</strong> <me>h = \frac{2A}{a + b}</me></p>
                <p><strong>(b)</strong> <me>h = \frac{2(30)}{4 + 6} = \frac{60}{10} = 6</me></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Given the formula for simple interest <m>I = \frac{PRT}{100}</m>:</p>
                <ol>
                    <li>Make <m>R</m> the subject of the formula.</li>
                    <li>Find <m>R</m> when <m>I = 200</m>, <m>P = 1000</m>, and <m>T = 4</m>.</li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><strong>(a)</strong> <me>R = \frac{100I}{PT}</me></p>
                <p><strong>(b)</strong> <me>R = \frac{100(200)}{1000 \times 4} = \frac{20000}{4000} = 5</me></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Given the equation for a straight line <m>3y + 2x = 12</m>:</p>
                <ol>
                    <li>Make <m>y</m> the subject of the formula.</li>
                    <li>Find <m>y</m> when <m>x = 3</m>.</li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><strong>(a)</strong> <me>y = \frac{12 - 2x}{3}</me></p>
                <p><strong>(b)</strong> <me>y = \frac{12 - 2(3)}{3} = \frac{12 - 6}{3} = \frac{6}{3} = 2</me></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Given the formula for the volume of a cylinder <m>V = \pi r^2 h</m>:</p>
                <ol>
                    <li>Make <m>h</m> the subject of the formula.</li>
                    <li>Find <m>h</m> when <m>V = 154</m>, <m>r = 7</m>, and <m>\pi \approx \frac{22}{7}</m>.</li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><strong>(a)</strong> <me>h = \frac{V}{\pi r^2}</me></p>
                <p><strong>(b)</strong> <me>h = \frac{154}{\frac{22}{7} \times 49} = \frac{154}{22 \times 7} = \frac{154}{154} = 1</me></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Given the relationship <m>F = \frac{9}{5}C + 32</m>:</p>
                <ol>
                    <li>Make <m>C</m> the subject of the formula.</li>
                    <li>Find <m>C</m> when <m>F = 68</m>.</li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><strong>(a)</strong> <me>C = \frac{5(F - 32)}{9}</me></p>
                <p><strong>(b)</strong> <me>C = \frac{5(68 - 32)}{9} = \frac{5(36)}{9} = 5 \times 4 = 20</me></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>

        <table>
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                    <cell>Strategy &amp; Learning Experience</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Provide numerical tracking sheets where letters are temporarily replaced with boxes, or use physical equation scales to demonstrate balancing inverse operations step-by-step.
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        <line>The core lesson activities as described above.</line>
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Task learners with changing the subject of formulas where the target variable appears more than once (e.g., <m>A = P(1 + rt)</m> to make <m>P</m> or <m>t</m> the subject), introducing factoring strategies.
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>
